op("THEORY-TS-CERT", date=2026-08-08) = {
  fuse:        "experiments/thermal_stress.vsm",
  phase:       "PH-02 THEORY",
  action:      "reclassify + certify",
  certified:   { date: 2026-08-08, rubric: "VSF-CERT-RUBRIC-v1", verdict: CERTIFIED,
    reclassified: "@status historical_record. It is ¬theory — it is the DESIGN of
      an experiment that has since run and closed (EXP-TSM-01 2026-04-20,
      EXP-TSM-65C 2026-04-27). It sat in theory/ with ∄ @status, so nothing
      marked it executed OR superseded, and it drifted from the kernel meanwhile",
    findings: [
      "CPU_PRESSURE SEMANTICS WRONG: the doc defined sv[CPU_PRESSURE] = temp_C/safety_temp_C and called it 'the FIRST real-hardware data feed in Block B'. CPU_PRESSURE is LINUX PSI — hardware_ingest.rs:41, /proc/pressure/cpu avg10. A named StateVector slot carrying the wrong semantics in the spec: anyone reading CPU_PRESSURE out of the ledger with this document in hand reads a temperature ratio where the kernel wrote scheduler pressure, and under an IO-bound load the two are ¬even correlated. Telemetry field renamed thermal_load — what it always was. ∄ kernel change",
      "SENSOR PRIORITY INVERTED: doc says PRIMARY hwmon5 (coretemp), FALLBACK thermal_zone9. Kernel says PRIMARY thermal_zone0 (acpitz), FALLBACK hwmon5 — and it was reordered DELIBERATELY by ENV-SENSOR-FIX on 2026-07-03 to close 19 FALSE-POSITIVE alerts, aligning the kernel with what S3* host_probe reads. The document still carried the pre-fix ordering: the one that produced the false positives. Also named zone9 where the kernel reads zone0",
      "A3 cited for BOTH vectors_max and safety_temp_C. A3 is B(L) = min(B_tokens, B_compute, B_time) — it bounds the compute budget, ¬a temperature. The thermal ceiling stands on the hardware limit, ¬on an axiom",
      "sensor paths + hwmon indices + node identity were hard-coded inline — which is exactly how the sensor divergence survived unnoticed. Now @thermal_primary / @thermal_fallback, with the ordering rationale on the binding"],
    added: "the OUTCOME the document never carried: both runs done, ∄ drift at 45°C or 65°C, senescence structurally zero — WITH the reading that matters: both held H_rest as a compile-time constant, so δ·ReLU(H_rest_drift) ≡ 0 BY CONSTRUCTION. The experiment could ¬have found drift. Law B is UNTESTED, ¬refuted" },
  verify:      { parser: "VALID", paths: "0 ▪ bindings 93 · dangling — · orphan —" },
}

